# Tagline Configuration

# This file is optional. Tagline works out of the box with sensible defaults
# for repos that follow conventional commits.
#
# Three sections below are parsed deterministically (look for `## Branches`,
# `## Pre-release Tags`, and `## Versioning`). Anything else in this file is
# passed to the AI as free-form context — write whatever a teammate would need
# to know about how your team releases.

## Branches

- production: main
- staging: staging
- development: develop

## Pre-release Tags

- staging suffix: rc
- development suffix: alpha

## Versioning

# Optional. Default is `semver`. Set this when your repo releases via
# calendar versioning or a simple incrementing counter instead.
#
# Schemes:
#   - semver       (default) — conventional-commits drive patch/minor/major math.
#   - calver       — pattern-driven from the current UTC date.
#   - incremental  — trailing integer +1 per release.
#
# Calver tokens: YYYY, YY, 0Y, MM, 0M, DD, 0D, MICRO. MICRO is required.

- scheme: semver
# - scheme: calver
# - pattern: YYYY.0M.MICRO

## Release Notes Style

Write release notes for a technical audience. Be concise.
Group changes under: New Features, Bug Fixes, Maintenance.
Highlight any security-related fixes prominently.
When a ticket number is present, include it in brackets after the item.

## Scope Notes

This is a Node.js API service. Changes to `src/` affect the public API.
Changes under `infra/` are infrastructure-only and should be noted as such.
The `packages/ui` package has its own release cycle — exclude from API releases.
